    <Snapshot Date="2026-08-28 06:44:28 -0400">
        <Title>Untitled Snapshot</Title>
        <Text>The wilderness is Solraheen’s garden. It is a sacred garden. Every littler garden is sacred, too. Mandri had seen his fair share of them. He’d walked under the royal palms of Lord Kineal, and he’d nearly drowned in the Lordling of Luzee’s terraced falls. He’d seen every garden that people boast about seeing, and they were all of them disgusting, those thirsty greenscapes.
There was always this focus on a stripe of atmosphere reflecting off the surface of a stream. This line represented death, or something like that. There might have been more to the metaphor, but Mandri knew the true meaning here: people built these gardens to appear gently born. And that was the charitable interpretation. The only other explanation was that they derived some perverse aristocratic pleasure from putting plants into their ‘proper places’. Bushes were stuck here, trees imprisoned there, flowering weeds were permitted to trim the walkways. A good slaughter every now and then ensured none of them ever rose up out of their station or class. It was a hierarchy of form, all kept in good order by the brilliance of the human mind. The chaos of nature? The lack of clear boundaries? Intolerable. A Hrkan garden was Nature as god did not intend, but everyone agreed, it was much prettier to look at. That meant it was Better.
Vigram’s garden had no placid stream to symbolize a ‘crossing over’ from life to death. There were no trees, or flowers, or artificial boundaries between them. No, when Mandri stepped through that ironwood gate and onto a plain of bone-white pebbles, he stepped into a garden composed entirely of curated stone.
As a garden, it was unique, but even so, it still obeyed all the rules. It was quick to establish a hierarchy of form, and so the stones came in a spectrum of sizes, from ox-sized to flaky specks. Each stone was arranged in such a way so as to draw the eye, or in this case, a person, through a maze of perception and towards what was important. Most rules of thumb in art have to do with manipulating contrast; sharp against soft; dark against light; so against not-so. Here, a rippling sea of smooth white pebbles flowed against jagged knots of granite, granite which grew in immensity towards the center of this snow-white field of rock, and there, in the god-spot center, stood a towering spike of Fabled Black.
That was something to behold—
Fabled Black.
The skewering stone.
Shard of the firmament.
Quintessence of night.
If it were a tree, and the pebbles, grass, and the granite, bushes, it all would have been too stilted, but this was a god-killing stone, and so the stasis, the general air of oppression, that was the point.
Mandri walked up to this black obelisk. He saw his own reflection in its surface sheen, split by streaks of rain. He looked worse than he’d ever been—eyes red and witless, face gaunt, lips cracked. His wet hair clung tight to his meatless skull. He was death. Perhaps everyone who looked into the depths of Fabled Black looked like death.
“The stone makes the most wondrous pigment,” Vigram said from behind. The big man looked longingly at the obelisk. “It makes a black which rivals the crow.” He joined Mandri and set a fat hand upon this dreadful spike, caressing it as if it were a prized horse. “It’s humbling to know that this is only a tiny sliver of the comet which killed Solraheen.”
“Isn’t it illegal?” Mandri asked. “I mean, to have this out in the open…how did you get it? Where did you find it?”
“Bribery is the answer to all your questions,” Vigram said flatly. “Provisions for the smuggling alone cost me thirty-five silver strings.”
“I see.”
“You can touch it if you like.”
“No thanks.”
“You are right, there’s no need.” Vigram pulled his hand away from the rock. “Merely being in its presence is enough.”
This was true. Fabled Black was notorious for its aura of doom. A small speck of it could make the happiest child feel rotten. Here in such quantity, that feeling crept down to the bone.
“Isn’t it strange,” Vigram said as he stared blankly at the clouds, “that everything which falls from the sky should radiate such sadness? This rain…this stone...I sometimes wonder if the heavens are a reservoir of despair and these elemental things are infused with it. Then I consider how desperate this world is, and I begin to believe that its the other way around, that the heavens are place a joy and those those things which fall from it long to return and escape this place. The rain would prefer to dance in the clouds. Wouldn’t this stone rather gird the stars than be stuck in this spot? I think so. I should say, I know this is true, because the Black is alive and I have seen what it can do. Would you like to see this? Come, join me in my home, and see.”
Vigram spared a beneficent smile as the light rain plinked his cheeks. His teeth did not sparkle as they once did. His beard was astonishingly regular. The stone, or the long march up the hill, was pushing Mandri’s mind back into sobriety. 
“Do I have a choice?” Mandri asked.
“Why do you ask?” Vigram said, frowning. “Do you feel threatened? I have never threatened you.”
“You might not have, but Lora...”
“Please,” Vigram said, heavy brow pinching. “Let us say that you have fulfilled any obligation she impressed upon you, and so you can leave if you wish. No harm will come to you, at least none that you do not inflict yourself. The choice is yours. I will rescind my invitation if you bring this tension into my house. But, if you are at all curious about what may lay in store for you, then admit it, and we shall find it out together.”
Vigram skirted around the spike of Black to find shelter under the roof of his palace’s portico. While Mandri dithered, the enormous palace doors opened, revealing a scrawny servant boy standing in the entry way, carrying towels and clean robes. This was enough to encourage Mandri to join Vigram on the stoop. To be sure, he was also curious, because if this house had a shard of Black sitting in its garden, then what was kept inside?
Mandri accepted the towel. He slipped out of his soggy robe, dried himself, then dressed himself in a fresh knee-length robe, white. Vigram’s new robe was also white, though instead of linen, it was made of silk. It shimmered like a rolling cloud as Vigram slipped into it.
“Before we enter,” Vigram said as he toweled his beard, “I want you to assert yourself. I won’t have any of your usual meekness in this house. I might have put up with it before, but stand up straight if you can. Sol’s light, you look like you’ve just been squeezed out the womb.”
Vigram tossed the towel to the servant boy then waited for Mandri to assert himself. Mandri, not knowing what to do, simply nodded.
“That will have to do,” Vigram said, and then he led Mandri inside, where the wide floor was white, the ceiling white, and the walls, corners, and trim, all white on white.
“It cost fifty silver strings to burn enough lime for the interior plaster,” Vigram remarked. “You may have noticed the walls outside, and they cost…well, I don’t want to go on about expenses.” He gave a smug and self-aware smile.
Mandri nodded along. Unlike the garden outside, this palace met expectations entirely. He could have been standing inside any number of palaces that scabbed the Hrkan world; tall arched ceilings, wide halls with skinny walls, no ornamentation, everything painted white. No color. The ostentation of color was to be abhorred in favor of natural light, Solraheen’s light. Paintings were the only exceptions, for these were sacred expressions as well, though every painting must be self-contained, bounded by a frame, for it was said that Solraheen would refuse to dwell in a place that presumed to blur the line between human and divine creation. 
Emptiness was the rule. Emptiness was desired. This was not at odds with the extravagant games that the rich play to prove their wealth, for if one is allowed to have only nothing, the question then becomes, how much nothing could one accumulate? The height of Vigram’s sway-away ceiling measured how much emptiness he could afford. Judge the floor—the richer man’s floor bears nothing, not even dirt. A truly rich man could afford to employ a person or persons to always be wiping away the filth tramped in from the street. Vigram’s floor was as white as the ceiling, and as Vigram and Mandri walked along barefoot, the door-boy crawled behind on hands and knees with a bowl of water and a long hog bristle brush, scrubbing away.
The narrow gallery was especially clean, and bright, too, courtesy of the ten or so sawtooth skylights which dotted the long hall. This strong ambient light preserved the integrity of the paintings in this gallery. It was quite the feat, considering the sun outside was occluded by clouds.
One painting in particular stood out, as it was set up a little higher than the others. It was Lokinvar’s, “Man and Woman at a Window”. It was a simple painting. There stands a young man and a young woman in front of a white wall, both well dressed in plain robes, with a tall, rectangular window between them. The window looks out on the city of Jeket. Solraheen’s temple figures prominently there, but the couple are not interested in the view, they eye each other. There are other figures milling about, walking out of frame, looking nowhere, obviously ignorant to the hands of the young man and young woman: the young man’s right hand, down at his side, is clutched into a fist, while the woman’s left hand is open, palm facing the man. The man’s fist is a secret invitation to a lover’s tryst, and the woman’s palm is eager acceptance. These hand signs were well known because the painting was well known. In fact, it was one of the most famous paintings in all the world. 
“How did you get a hold of this?” Mandri wondered aloud. “And don’t say bribery. I saw this two years ago, sitting in the house of Kist.”
“Oh, it was no trouble,” Vigram said. “I wanted it, and so I have it.”
“You stole it?”
“Yes! It’s the greatest work of art I’ve ever seen.”
“Is it?”
“Mandri, you have this habit of mocking me discreetly, as if I don’t notice. You should do so openly. Assert yourself.”
“I wasn’t mocking you. I simply disagree. I think this is the least interesting painting here.”
“But you haven’t looked at any of the others.”
“I don’t need to.”
“Could you have painted the subtle satin patterns woven into their robes? I doubt it. The figure, as well: the subtlety in expression is second to none. I should say, the couple feel so real they description. All that can be said is that any man who sees it will fall in love with the lady, and ladies will fall for the gentleman, such is their vitality. That a human being could have painted this seems impossible. That is the real power in the work—the awareness that a human being painted this. A silly, stupid human being rose above his baser instincts for long enough to produce an object beyond himself. For that reason, it is the greatest painting in all the world.”
“The greatest?” Mandri asked with slight acid bite.
Vigram chuckled. “You are merely put off by being in the presence of skill greater than your own. I know that you agree with me.”
“What is there to agree with? You have your preference, I have mine. That’s all there is to it.”
Vigram stroked his beard. “This is not a matter of preference, Mandri. This painting is the greatest painting in the world. This is an objective fact.”
“I was under the impression that these things were a matter of taste.”
“Go outside, gather one hundred people, parade them through this room, and ninety of them will agree that this painting is the best work in the house.”
“What would that prove?”
“It would prove the work’s superiority.”
“What if I trust my own opinion more than theirs?”
“That would only prove that you have been corrupted by a degenerate culture.” 
Mandri laughed. “Vigram,” he said as he tried and utterly failed to hide a mocking smile, “These are opinions. I have my opinion, and the hundred people you would march through here each have their own opinion. These are all subjective opinions. You’re going to have to explain to me how adding a bunch of subjective opinions together makes them an objective measure.” 
“It simply does. Consider—there is a reason, a fundamental reason, why this painting is known throughout the world, and none of yours are.”
“Of course there’s a reason. It was stolen five times in the last hundred years. Two hundred years ago, and you can look into this yourself, two hundred years ago this painting was listed somewhere in the middle of Lokinvar’s works. Then it got stolen, that generated interest, the hype took off from there.”
“Why was it stolen? It was stolen because there is something about the work which transcends—”
“Have you considered that it was stolen because it was easy to steal?”
“This was not easy to steal.”
“You actually stole it?”
Vigram smiled grandly. “I already admitted this, yes! How else was I going to get it? What was I supposed to do? They had it sitting in a retrospective!”
“What’s wrong with that?”
“To sit a Lokinvar next to a Lokinvar, where is the context? One needs to see the distance between him, and every other painter in order to properly appreciate…this is another objective measure by the way, as objective as any other measurement of distance.”
“But people prefer different distances, don’t they?”
“What does that even mean? Be reasonable, Mandri. I’m beginning to believe that you are arguing  only to argue.”
“What if I am? Why do you care if I think the same way that you do? If Solraheen wanted us all to think the same things, believe the same things, if He wanted us to look at the world in the same way with objective measures, then why make us all different?”
“Why indeed,” Vigram mused. His smile took on a glimmer of satisfaction, as if Mandri was on the cusp of some great realization. “I want you to consider two facts,” he said. He held out his hands as if to weigh these two oncoming facts. “First: we are all different. Second: it was never supposed to be that way.” He then clapped his hands. The loud slap summoned a slave from some narrow orifice in the wall. This slave carried a shallow bowl lined with finger-length wraps of freeweed. “Take a wrap,” said Vigram, “and consider those two facts together.”
Mandri declined with a shrug.
“No, I suppose you don’t need it,” Vigram said. “You are remarkably calm. You do have that hopeless look about you, but I always supposed that was the general malaise of the indigent.” Vigram selected a wrap for himself, tucked it into the breast of his robe, then waved his slave away. “We’ll save the gallery tour for another time. I have you here for a purpose, after all.”
“About that,” Mandri countered. “I can tell you right now that I’m not going to kill anyone for you. If that’s what this is about. Or, do you want me to steal something?”
Vigram brushed Mandri off with a polite wave. “You cannot tell me anything about what you want to do. I should say that I know more about you than you do.”
“I doubt that.”
“Try me.”
“What is my mother’s name?”
“I don’t pollute my mind with trifles,” Vigram snorted. “Neither do you. Consider—do you know the personal name of a lizard? No? But you do know more about a lizard than it knows about itself. I would hope. Your knowledge of the lizard circumscribes the minutiae of its life to paint a more complete picture. That is how knowledge works. I should go as far to say that knowledge is an advanced species of ignorance. We ignore choice details so as to avoid becoming swamped in a tide of them.” 
Mandri quirked his head in confusion, because that was the maximum amount of asserting himself he felt comfortable with.
“I can see that you are going to need it explained,” Vigram said. “Come. We must study at least one more piece of art. Come.”
Vigram led Mandri through a series of rooms, each with a ceiling higher than the one before. To demonstrate the great expense of having nothing in a world where things naturally accumulate, the place was sparsely furnished. If there was a table, it was carved from a pale slab of sandstone. If there was a chair, its legs were sanded branches and its seat was woven reed. If there was a tapestry, it was blank white, for its purpose was to be a canvas for the subtleties of Solraheen’s light, which today, was cold, gray, and flattening.
Vigram led Mandri into a cavernous room that had a circular hole in its roof. A light rain suffused with silver sunlight streamed through this hole to pitter-patter into a rainwater pool. All edges of the room were laden with pillows, all of them white, with white tassels. On the opposite side of the sprinkling rain was a fresco that spanned the entirety of the wall. Heretically, there was no frame, though it was a magnificent work of art in skill and in subject. It depicted Solraheen at the very moment of His victory over evil. He was there, stretched out, as if flying, and his face wore a beatific smile, clearly pleased with the sight of His sword as He plunged it into His brother’s back. In the classic Hrkan style, Solraheen was pure white with a hrkan glow—red, green, and blue—while His brother, Schisol, was an ashy black. The swarm of people surrounding them were a whirlwind cast in every color imaginable.
“I know I spoke highly of Lokinvar, but this,” Vigram said contentedly, “is my favorite piece. For sentimental reasons, mostly. The god of creation destroying the god of destruction. Shouldn’t it be the other way around? It has so much behind it, if you know what I mean.”
“I don’t.”
“See this yellow?” Vigram said, admiring. “Isn’t it beautiful?” He pointed to the golden rays which emanated out from Solraheen’s head. It truly was a fantastic yellow, extraordinarily vibrant, as if it had been painted on mere moments ago. “Can you guess the pigment?”
“I have no idea.”
“This yellow, it comes from the Gabo cow. Incredibly expensive. The most wasteful pigment imaginable. To make enough for this fresco I had to buy three men to care for fifty cows. I had to put them on a strict diet, the cows. They couldn’t eat anything but this little squeaking bug, called a zuberant. They look like locusts, thick and stubby like your thumb, well, my thumb.”
“I’ve seen them.”
“You buy zuberants in bulk. Then you make a paste out of them, and feed it to the cows. Their bellies have to be stuffed full all at once. That means you have to starve them a bit so they take to that bug-paste like dogs to the bone. The next step must be timed correctly, because, you see, this radiant pigment comes from the chyme of the cow’s third stomach, so you must be precise with the cutting. You must wait until the chyme extrudes into that stomach, not the first or second stomach or the color won’t leap. It’s the third stomach, and that’s when you cut open the beast and harvest as much of the yellow slime as you can. Then, you dry it out into a powder, and that is that. Once you have the powder, you can sell the meat. I don’t sell the meat. I let it rot in the sun.”
“Why?”
“Because it feels…” Vigram waved his hand in the air as if to catch the words that could accurately describe what he was thinking. “It feels weighty to have killed a herd of beasts for the explicit purpose of painting my wall. Doesn’t Solraheen look radiant? And now that you know fifty cows died to make it seem so, doesn’t that make the fresco take on a more…oh, I can’t find the words. It feels weighty. It feels more...”
“It doesn’t do anything for me.”
Vigram’s round face lit up. “Hah! It doesn’t, does it?! You can’t judge me, boy, no one can. The gods are all dead. Here are the last of them, Schisol and Solraheen, and there—” he pointed to the top of the piece, to the screaming black comet in the sky, “There is the stone of Fabled Black coming to end them both. Such a fine image, no? The quality of the painting is sublime, yes?”
“Sure.”
“Don’t be flippant. Assert yourself. It is beautiful. This is Ellag’s hand. I trust you are familiar with her work?”
“I am.”
“She is here, you know. I keep her here to help me find my Mazid. I should say that this is why you are here. I am seeking a Mazid, and I am to be its Minder.”
“That’s what this is about?”
Vigram’s demeanor shifted into something more serious as he turned his attention away from the fresco and towards Mandri alone. “Tell me, Mandri, do you know what a Mazid is?”
“Of course. Everybody knows.”
“What does everybody know?”
Vigram’s question suggested the man was seeking some deeper truth about a Mazid’s nature than mere function and appearance. Mandri had seen a few of the Mazidai from a distance, as they were never far from a Hrkan Lord. They all had a certain slightness of size, and all of them were shrouded in dark robes and never spoke. Mandri had only ever seen one of them up close. It was a young woman, underfed, and her serene expression was couched in generic Hrkan features—thin lips and sad eyes, all in near-genderless proportion. She was completely unremarkable except for the fact that she could shoot a scorching beam of golden fire out of her mouth.
“Everybody knows,” Mandri said, “that a Mazid is a weapon.”
Vigram relaxed, as if disappointed. “That is one role a Mazid fulfills,” he said, “But it is better to think of the Mazidai as the tools which Solraheen grants the Lordship to help them rearrange the world, to organize the world, to paint, if I may, a brighter future.”
“Right,” said Mandri, remembering. “A Mazid is meant to destroy evil.”
Vigram chuckled as if Mandri had just said something precious and absurd. “Tell me, Mandri, how can something which does not exist be destroyed? Consider—can you point to evil? I can point to you, I can point to this wall, but I cannot point to ‘evil’.” With that, Vigram put his fat finger up on the fresco and poked Schisol’s belly, right where Solraheen’s sword burst forth. A click sounded from under the floor, and a square of stone at the base of the wall levered open to reveal a narrow stairway descending into darkness. 
“Before we head down,” Vigram said, “I must demand from you your utmost commitment. I, unlike the Solitants, value honesty, so my good character compels me to warn you that there is a chance—a chance—that as we attempt to squeeze your Spark for ripeness, you will die. It is only a chance, and a small one at that, but I want you to consider that thought before you join me.”
“Why would I join you?” Mandri asked. “What’s in it for me?”
“It’s the same with everything in life—what’s in it for you is something you must make for yourself. All I can say is that the experience will be…well, I’m sure you know that no one is closer to a god than a Mazid. It is said to be a permanent high.”
Vigram hiked up the hem of his silky white robe and disappeared down into the inky blackness which shrouded the stairwell’s descent.
Mandri stared down that stairwell, and he must have stared for quite a while because Vigram’s voice echoed up, saying, “I said consider the thought, not inhabit it!”
Without really knowing what he was doing, Mandri stepped down the stairs.
A slave girl bustled out of obscurity to close the hatch behind him.
At the bottom of the stairs was a narrow hall, and at the end of this hall was a squarish room with a low ceiling. No expensive plaster here. In the room’s far right corner was a coal fire smoldering in a bronze brazier. Its red glow splayed out over three three wooden chairs set in the room’s middle, with two chairs facing one. Vigram sat in one of the two, and sitting in the chair next to him was a person draped in a white sheet.
“Don’t look so frightened,” Vigram sighed. “I told you not to come down if you weren’t committed. You have come, and now you are here, yes?”
Mandri nodded.
“Good. Now sit down.” 
Mandri took the remaining seat, across from Vigram and the veiled stranger.
Vigram pulled the wrap of freeweed from his pocket, then stretched to light it in the brazier’s coals. He puffed it thrice to get it going.
“So!” he said as he slapped his leg to capture Mandri’s attention. “Are you to be my Mazid? Or, are you simply a painter who is disillusioned with the craft of painting? You’d be surprised how many of those we find.”
Vigram puffed on his wrap.
Mandri simply sat there.
“Are your wits here, Mandri? Are you here?”
Mandri nodded.
“Use your voice. Assert yourself.”
Mandri cleared his throat, then nodded towards the sheeted person. “Who is that?”
“This is Ellag,” Vigram said. “Or, I should say, it was Ellag. She is dead. As I mentioned earlier, I keep her here to aid my search for a Mazid. I am looking for someone who understands what it means to be inspired. I find that Ellag’s presence helps to demonstrate the truth of what that means.”
Vigram’s chair creaked as he laid back into it. He blew a puff of smoke out the side of his beard, as if the smoke could go anywhere in this cramped room. Between the freeweed and the smoking coals, Mandri was having a hard time finding a clean breath.
Vigram crossed his tree-trunk legs. “Tell me, Mandri, what do you think Art is? If you could define it. Define Art.”
Mandri eyed the sheeted woman. “What does that have to with her?”
“Don’t be coy, Mandri. This is important. You must be eased into the right frame of mind before we move on to the test itself.”
“I’m more concerned about what’s under that sheet.”
“Is that why you don’t want to paint anymore, you think there are more important things to be doing?”
“At the moment, yes.”
“Come now, you’ve spent too much time in front of an easel not to have some idea of what it is that you are doing. What is art? What does an artist do?”
Mandri felt he was stumbling into a trap. Or maybe he was already trapped and he didn’t know it. He stuttered and said, “An artist makes art.”
Vigram sighed. He took a long drag from his wrap. He held the smoke in his great big chest, and when he finally exhaled, his round shoulders slumped so far it seemed the essence of his life had left him. “You think art is merely a word,” he said. “You think that it is a label that people slap onto things for utility.”
“I didn’t say that.”
“Then say something! I’m trying to help you avoid ending up like Ellag here.” He put his arm around Ellag’s shoulder and gave her body a friendly shake. Her sheeted head rolled limply and then settled onto his chest as though she were looking to cuddle. The sheet pulled taught over her face and a smear of blood welled up from her forehead to stain the fibers.
Vigram narrowed his eyes at Mandri. The air between them swirled with smoke. 
“Answer my question,” Vigram commanded.
“Is she dead?” Mandri squeaked.
“I’ve already told you, yes! She is dead! Answer the question.”
“What was the question?” 
Vigram sighed again, then steadied Ellag back onto her own chair so that he could lean forward and free his hands for explanation. He said, “Ellag thought that art was about the artist. A person goes out into the world and has some incredible experience—like you’re having now. It changes them in some important, fundamental way. They look at the world differently. But how could any of their friends or family know that any of this has occurred? The change is internal, it is not written on the skin. There might be some new sparkle in the eye, their behavior might be different, but none of that will ever communicate the depth of what has happened. Consider a woman crying—for what reason does she cry? She tells you her child has died, but you’ve never had a child, Mandri, so how could you understand? To understand, you would have to have experienced it for yourself. This is the problem which art purports to solve. A work of art provides a surrogate experience which allows us to break through that impenetrable barrier between our private experiences.”
Not one word of that made it to Mandri’s ear. He was too focused on finding a chance to flee. He suspected could make a dash for the stairs with a head start, because Vigram was so wrapped up in the freeweed’s smoke.
“That’s what Ellag thought,” Vigram said. “She thought that art is a thing an artist does to let everyone else know what’s going on inside. Does that make sense?”
“It’s very sentimental.”
“Sentimental, yes, that would be a word for it. Unfortunately, it is completely and utterly wrong. Look how wrong it can be.”
Vigram bent down, grabbed the foot of Ellag’s sheet, and in one quick flourish he flipped it over the back of Ellag’s chair to reveal her corpse.
Ellag’s dead head bobbed and settled, and as it did a trickle of blood dripped out of the empty sockets that had once held her eyes. The belly of her tunic had been ripped apart, while the belly itself had been carved open with surgical precision, skin folded aside like pages from a book to reveal a cavity under her ribs—emptiness through to her spine. She was hollowed out. 
“Steady, Mandri. Steady yourself…now, looking at Ellag, you might suppose that I think I am an artist, and that this,” he put his hand on Ellag’s skull and twisted it about in a perverse display of puppetry, “this is me communicating to you in a way that lets you experience, in a profound way, who I am and what it is like to be me.”
He let go of Ellag’s head. It slumped down onto her collarbone.
“But that would be a misunderstanding. Consider—why isn’t speech, casual conversational speech like we’re having now, why isn’t that considered to be art? I am manipulating my throat, tongue, teeth, and lips to shape a string of sounds for you to experience. Sentences are things that can break down that barrier between our lived experiences, yes? The more skilled you are at this, the better you are at the art of speech, yes?”
At this point the freeweed was having its way with Vigram. His gesticulations grew frenetic. He cut the air with a slicing hand and declared, “No! I am not an artist. I am many things, but I am not an artist.”
“You’re a murderer.”
“I’m not a murderer, either.”
Vigram took another big drag from his freeweed.
“Actually,” Vigram said, “that’s not true, I am a murderer. But, I think you are referring to Ellag, here. I did not kill her. She died because she thought art was about the artist. She thought that the painter was important. She thought far too much of herself.” He churned his words into the air with his free hand, as if mixing his musings with the smoke would bring out some flavor of the truth.
“You’re insane,” said Mandri.
“It is good that you should say that. Ellag was never able to make that step. She was too…embodied. Like this bundle of freeweed. She was also frightened.” He leaned forward a little in his seat. “See, if you look into the Black when you are frightened, you are bound to see frightening things.” 
Vigram made an explosion with his fingers, pretending to burst his eyes.
“Her eyes exploded?” Mandri asked in wonder.
“They did. You may see the stain there on the floor. It was fascinating.”
“What about her stomach?”
“That was me,” Lora said, sneaking up from behind, spooking Mandri near out of his skin. The old witch drifted by, a tall wisp wrapped in black, and stood beside Vigram. Her cowled head nearly brushed the ceiling. “I wished to query the girl’s stomach.”
“All done after the fact,” Vigram assured. “But, we shouldn’t get distracted.” He poked these words with his wrap of freeweed, and this shook a clump of ash free from the glowing tip.
“No, we should not,” Lora agreed. “We should begin.” 
“Of course.”
Vigram set his freeweed onto the corner of his seat, then stood up. He moseyed over to the far side of the room, hidden by Ellag’s shadow. There he struggled with a square stone slab which was as black as the darkness from which he pulled it. He propped the massive thing up against his thighs and waddled it over. He laid it down flat at Mandri’s feet, then shuffled back into the dark to fetch three three clay jars. These, he set next to the black stone panel. Each jar was filled with a black paint.
Lora pulled from her robe a paintbrush. Its handle was short compared to the length of the brush head, which was as long as Mandri’s forearm. It looked more like a whip than a paintbrush.
“Paint a picture,” she said as she handed the brush over.
“A picture of what?”
“If I may,” Vigram interjected.
Lora grunted, then stood back to give Vigram room.
Vigram crouched to evenly meet Mandri’s eye. “Remember what I said about the true artist being a person of excess? Back at the leech farm?”
Mandri nodded.
“Well,” Vigram said, “I lied. The opposite is true. The true artist is empty. So, do not think about what you should paint. Do not give yourself that privilege. Do not think about what should or shouldn’t be. You need simply to start painting.”
“You want me to figure things out as I go?”
“No. Do not think. You are merely a tool. True art creates itself. It is reckoned with on its own terms. It is its own experience. It communicates nothing but what it is. You kneel there,” he pointed to the floor in front of Mandri, “and you become the tool by which the work creates itself.”
Mandri squinted in confusion.
If he thought about nothing, then he would paint nothing.
Even if he wanted to paint something, this dim light made it impossible to see what he was doing. And there were only the three black paints to put on a black slab of rock.
Vigram returned to his seat, and the sheer weight of him plopping onto his too-small chair disturbed Ellag’s corpse, which slumped forward, limp wrists smacking onto the floor.
“This test,” Vigram said thoughtfully, “will determine how skilled you are at removing yourself from yourself. Now, we already know you are quite skilled at this. It is why you are here.” 
Mandri nodded as if Vigram was making sense. He knelt down before the black stone slab and thought about what he should do. Perhaps, if he swirled some of the paint on the slab, Vigram would let him leave.
With no palette, the stone itself would have to be the palette. It wasn’t like he was going to run into trouble mixing paints if the only color was black. So, one by one, he picked up the clay jars and, trembling, poured their contents onto the stone surface.
The first paint came out of its jar in a clump, a sand gel slopping onto the stone. Little granules of Fabled Black inside this gel caught the firelight and sparkled.
The next paint was a clean, syrupy goo that drizzled out of its jar.
The last paint was so thin that Mandri had to be careful pouring it out, lest it splash into his lap. Thankfully, the surface of the stone was ever so slightly bowled. When he was done with all this, he cast a side-eyed glance at Vigram to see if the man approved of what he was doing.
Vigram glared back. “Don’t look at me, look at the work.”
Mandri’s breathing grew nervous.
The smoke was stifling.
He fanned his tunic. Light as the linen was, the room's heat had become unbearable.
Vigram was looking for him to do a specific something, and if he didn’t do it, he would not be allowed to leave. The fear this realization evinced was so strong it was paralyzing.
“What will happen if I fail?” Mandri asked.
Vigram shook his head. “Think, instead, of what will happen should you succeed. If you can let yourself be used by the work, think of what you can become!”
“What should I paint?”
Vigram took another drag of his freeweed and reclined back into his chair. He looked away and studied his own smoke, pretending he’d lost all interest in Mandri and the Black slab on the floor.
Lora stood resolute.
The time for talk was over.
Mandri turned his attention to his task. 
What could he possibly paint with Black, on top of Black, on top of Black?
He poked the long-haired brush into the sandy gel and spread it around. It did not mix with the other paints, indeed it pushed right through them. The thinner paints in turn flooded into the grooves left by the brush’s passing.
He sat back on his heels and studied the whorl he’d made. The sheen of the red-orange firelight played with each paint differently. It stuck softly to the fine grained gel, it stippled the syrupy fluid, and it hardly touched the thinnest paint. Wherever the thinnest paint pooled, the image was truly Black.
Mandri pushed and pulled the paints around to build an image out of the light, as a sculptor might build an image out of shadows. He didn’t much care about what he was making, the brush hairs were too long and too limp to make deliberate marks. All he knew was that he was painting something.
Black pools welled up like the hollows of a human skull.
A face.
Why not?
He pulled a stripe of shadow to form up the upper lip. He pushed a mound of the gel together to bring out the cheeks. It was coming along. A long and powerful face. He swirled the brush around in an eye socket, trying to find the eye. Suddenly, there it was, an eye of firelight.
It blinked.
Mandri startled backwards in fright, but so compelling was this vision that he quickly gathered himself and peered back down at the image in disbelief. Yes, this face was alive. A thin stream of black ran down below the upper lip, opening the mouth.
Was there a tongue in there? A black snake of a tongue glinting in the back of the throat?
Yes!
“Do you see this?” Mandri asked, but no response came.
He looked up to see if Vigram was even looking, but the man wasn’t there. Neither was Lora. Neither were there chairs, or even the walls, there was only himself, the blazing brazier, his painting, and a void.
The face he’d painted began to extrude out of the stone. An old woman’s face, tortured, grieving, a hag.
Mandri scrambled backwards on all fours.
When the head was fully in the round, it tilted upright and cried tears of Black. Then the face sloughed off, slapped back down against the stone, and now floating there, eye level, was a Black sphere.
Mandri screamed, but no sound came. And now, he could not close his mouth.
The Black sphere flew straight at his open mouth. It smacked into his teeth and nose, and exploded into a splash into Black paint. The force of it threw him backwards, and while he tumbled, the paint swirled down his throat. He tumbled and tumbled, until finally, he tumbled right out of the void.
He landed hard on stone.
He choked and sputtered.
Then came an agonized scream.
“AAAAAAAAAARGH!”
He woke up sprawled against the wall in Vigram’s basement. Vigram himself was kneeling nearby, distressed. The man’s eyes were wet. His teeth bit hard on his fraying freeweed wrap.
“This pissweed hardly numbs it!” Vigram cried.
“Quiet yourself,” Lora said as she hovered over the big man’s shoulder. Her long black knife dripped blood. Vigram’s severed hand lay in a dark puddle between them. Vigram noticed Mandri staring staring at it.
“You saw it!” Vigram shrieked.
“He swallowed it,” Lora added.
Vigram glanced at the painted slab behind him, then muttered a single, breathless word, “Incredible...”
Mandri tried to sit up, but Vigram roared, then threw his great weight down upon him, pinning him against the ground.
“Hold still!” Vigram groaned as his one good hand gripped Mandri’s throat. His other arm squirted blood onto Mandri’s forehead with every thumping heartbeat. “You…must…hold…still!” His plumping smile savored every desperate wheeze. “I knew your Spark was ripe the moment I saw you.”
A few juddering moments later, and Mandrameen went limp.</Text>
